ASSERT(3)                      P32W Unix                       ASSERT(3)

NAME
       assert - program verification

SYNOPSIS
       #include <assert.h>

       assert(expression);

DESCRIPTION
       assert is a macro that checks that expression is true (non-
       zero) where the program says it must be. If it is false,
       assert writes

           assertion failed: expression, file line n

       to stderr, with the text of the expression and the source
       file and line, and calls abort(3), which ends the program
       with exit status 134.

       If NDEBUG is defined when assert.h is included, assert does
       nothing and expression is not evaluated, so expression must
       not have side effects the program needs. assert.h may be
       included again, with NDEBUG changed, to switch checking on
       or off for part of a file. The macro calls the library
       function __assert(expr, file, line).

EXAMPLES
       assert(n >= 0 && n < NSLOT);

       cc -DNDEBUG -o prog prog.c      (checks compiled out)

SEE ALSO
       cc(1), exit(3)

BUGS
       abort leaves no core, and output still buffered in streams
       other than stdout is lost. The
       message does not name the function. There is no
       static_assert.
